### The short version of how seventeen years of "unrelated" diagnoses turned out to be one story, told here so the rest of this series doesn't require homework.

> *Originally published on Substack. Imported to My Ledger as part of the adrenal journey series.*

If you came here from the Dispatches, you may already know some of this from a piece called "Fifteen Years, Six Specialists, Zero Questions." That one is still up in full. This is the short version, so this series has its own starting point.

For seventeen years I collected diagnoses. High blood pressure. Type 2 diabetes. High cholesterol. Sleep apnea. Vein problems in my twenties, the kind that usually show up decades later. Cataracts way earlier than they're supposed to happen. Cold hands, and hot everywhere else at the wrong times. Every one of them got its own doctor and its own prescription. Nobody ever stepped back and asked if five or six "unrelated" problems in the same man might have one cause. That wasn't anybody's job. Their job was to treat the one thing in front of them, in the fifteen minutes they had, and move on to the next patient. Most of them did that job well.

I started telling doctors back in 2010-2011 that something was driving it all. It wasn't just piling up one piece at a time. For years nobody listened, and nobody looked past whatever symptom was on the table that day. I got sideways with doctors over it twice before I finally insisted on expanded testing myself. I don't like admitting that was necessary. But that's how it works in this country. You push, or you wait.

What finally broke it open was a cardiologist looking at bloodwork outside his specialty. He told me the pattern looked like something nobody had connected. He also told me he didn't have time to run it down in a cardiology appointment, so I needed to go figure it out myself, and he told me which resources to use. So I did. I used AI to help me organize seventeen years of scattered test results into something that finally made sense. That led to a test nobody had ever ordered. That test led to another one, and that one led to imaging. The imaging found something real: masses on both of my adrenal glands. Not small ones, either. The size suggested they'd been there for years, maybe decades. It wasn't a hunch anymore. It was something on a scan a doctor could point to.

That's where this chapter ends. Since then I've met with an endocrine surgery team about what comes next, and that's what the rest of this series covers. It lives here in My Ledger, not in the Dispatches. The Dispatches are where I go after corporations and government. This is personal, so it goes here.

A few things before I go on. I'm not writing this for sympathy. I'm not looking for medical advice, either. I've got people helping me with what comes next. I'm writing it because the problem under my story isn't just mine. The system is built on fifteen-minute visits and doctors who each handle one organ, and nobody gets paid to look at the whole picture. I'd bet money some of you have your own version of this sitting in your chart right now, and nobody's asked about it. If any of this sounds familiar, it should.

Chapter 2 is next: the surgical consult, a committee I never sat in on, and what happened the day after. I'll go light on the clinical stuff and heavy on the honesty, same as always.
